\title{Score Before You Select: A Selection-Conditioned Evaluation Bias in Merit-Based Sortition}

\author{Anonymous Author(s)}
\affiliation{\institution{Anonymous Institution}\country{}}

\begin{abstract}
Merit-based sortition selects the active participants of a decentralized network by
ranking a smoothed record of their observed quality. Unobserved participants are credited
with the $P$-th percentile of the active set's observations, so $P$ controls turnover. While
extending this mechanism, we found that a natural evaluation metric, which scores the newly
selected set using the observations that selected it, is biased in a way that inverts
conclusions. We prove the bias is non-negative for any selection rule that is monotone in
a participant's own observation, and that it vanishes when no participant is at the
selection margin. Empirically it grows linearly with churn, at the same rate ($0.107$ to
$0.108$ per unit of churn) in percentile sortition and in an unrelated
upper-confidence-bound rule. Because churn rises with $P$, the bias moves the apparent
optimum from $P=20$--$30\%$ to $70$--$85\%$. Acting on it forfeits $35$--$48\%$ of the
mechanism's gain over random sortition, and it inverts the ranking of competing mechanisms
in all four pool dynamics we test. Measured correctly, volatility-adaptive $P$ is
statistically equivalent to a fixed $P$ at the same average in all $92$ variant--pool
combinations and under latent drift, because its control signal fluctuates at the
sampling-noise floor. \pending{headroom sentence} We close with an evaluation protocol for
selection mechanisms: score before you select.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{sortition, decentralized systems, evaluation methodology, selection bias, equivalence testing, reproducibility}

