\section{Introduction}

Decentralized networks that reward measurable work must repeatedly decide which
participants are \emph{active}. Merit-based sortition~\cite{kruijssen2024} makes that
decision by ranking an exponential moving average (EMA) of each participant's observed
quality. Participants who are not active produce no observations, so they are credited
with an imputed value: the $P$-th percentile of the active set's current observations. The
percentile $P$ is the mechanism's main free parameter. Kruijssen et al.\ find an optimum
at $P=20$--$40\%$ and recommend $P=25\%$ as a starting point.

We set out to extend this mechanism. Real networks are non-stationary, so it seemed
natural that $P$ should respond to the volatility of the active set, replacing participants
more aggressively when the network is unstable. Our first evaluation pipeline supported
that hypothesis strongly. This paper explains why it was wrong.

The error was not in the mechanism but in the measurement. To score each epoch, our
pipeline recorded the mean quality of the set that selection had \emph{just chosen}, using
the same observations that had driven the choice. Participants are retained \emph{because}
their latest observation was high, so the recorded score is conditioned on the selection
event. This is a domain instance of a familiar failure, the optimizer's
curse~\cite{smith2006optimizer} or ``double dipping''~\cite{kriegeskorte2009circular}.
What makes it dangerous here is a specific feature: the size of the bias grows with
participant turnover, and turnover grows with $P$. The bias is therefore correlated with
the very parameter under study, and it masquerades as a treatment effect.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item We characterise the bias. We prove it is non-negative for any selection rule that
is monotone in a participant's own score, show it vanishes exactly when no participant is
at the selection margin, and measure it as $0.108\times$ the churn rate ($R^2=0.874$).
An independent mechanism class, upper-confidence-bound selection, shows the same bias per
unit of churn ($0.107$), so the effect is a property of selection itself, not of one
mechanism (Section~\ref{sec:bias}).
\item We quantify what it invalidates. Tuning $P$ on the biased metric selects
$P\in[70,85]\%$ instead of $[20,30]\%$ and forfeits $35$--$48\%$ of the mechanism's entire
quality gain over random sortition. It also inverts the ranking of competing mechanisms in
all four pool dynamics we test (Section~\ref{sec:consequences}).
\item We re-evaluate volatility-adaptive $P$ correctly and find it statistically
\emph{equivalent} to a fixed $P$ at the same average, in all $92$ combinations of $23$
variants and four pools, and also under latent drift. The cause is measurable: the control
signal fluctuates at the sampling-noise floor of a standard-deviation estimator
(Section~\ref{sec:vams}). We also bound two-phase schedules
for $P$ with a hindsight oracle (Section~\ref{sec:headroom}).
\item We distil an evaluation protocol for selection mechanisms: score before selecting,
check against latent ground truth, tune on held-out seeds, and state null results as
equivalence tests (Section~\ref{sec:protocol}).
\end{enumerate}

We stress that the biased metric was ours, not Kruijssen et al.'s. Their metric is the
unbiased one, and measuring correctly \emph{reproduces} their recommended range. The
contribution is a documented failure mode, its mechanism, and the size of the error it
produces, so that others extending this family of mechanisms do not repeat it.

