\subsection{Related work}
\emph{Sortition} originally denotes random selection of representatives; recent work
designs selection algorithms with fairness guarantees~\cite{flanigan2021fair}, and
blockchains use cryptographic sortition to select committees~\cite{gilad2017algorand}.
Merit-based sortition~\cite{kruijssen2024} departs from both by weighting selection
toward measured performance while keeping a path back into the active set for every
participant.

The bias we report belongs to a well-studied family. Choosing the best of several noisy
estimates and then reporting that estimate overstates the true value: the winner's curse
in auctions~\cite{thaler1988winner}, the optimizer's curse in decision
analysis~\cite{smith2006optimizer}, circular analysis in
neuroimaging~\cite{kriegeskorte2009circular}, and optimistic bias from model selection in
machine learning~\cite{cawley2010overfitting}. Our contribution is not the phenomenon but
an instance with a property that makes it unusually harmful: its magnitude is a function
of the independent variable, so it changes the \emph{location} of the optimum rather than
adding a constant offset.

Our statistical protocol follows work on reproducibility in reinforcement
learning~\cite{henderson2018deep,agarwal2021precipice}, which shows that conclusions drawn
from few seeds are fragile. For null results we use two one-sided
tests~\cite{schuirmann1987tost,lakens2017equivalence}, which can affirmatively establish
that an effect is smaller than a stated threshold.

