\section{A Selection-Conditioned Bias}
\label{sec:bias}

\subsection{Three measurements}
Let $\mathrm{lat}(S)=|S|^{-1}\sum_{j\in S}\tilde Q_j$ be the mean latent quality of a set
$S$. An operator cares about the latent quality of the set it fields. Three per-epoch
measurements present themselves:
\begin{align}
M_1 &= \tfrac{1}{|A_i|}\textstyle\sum_{j\in A_i} T_{ij}
  && \text{score the fielded set, before selecting;}\\
M_2 &= \tfrac{1}{|A_{i+1}|}\textstyle\sum_{j\in A_{i+1}} \tilde T_{ij}
  && \text{score the newly selected set;}\\
M_3 &= \mathrm{lat}(A_{i+1})
  && \text{latent ground truth (simulation only).}
\end{align}
In $M_2$, $\tilde T_{ij}=T_{ij}$ for retained participants $j\in A_i\cap A_{i+1}$, and a
fresh independent draw for newly promoted ones, who have no observation yet. $M_1$ is the
``mean instantaneous quality metric across the active set'' of~\cite{kruijssen2024}. $M_2$
is the metric our own first-pass pipeline implemented. It looks reasonable: it reports the
quality of the set the mechanism has just chosen. Its defect is that the choice has
already consumed $T_{ij}$.

\subsection{The bias is non-negative, and vanishes without a margin}
Let $\mathcal H_i$ denote the history before the epoch-$i$ draws, including $A_i$ and all
latent qualities. Write $N'=|A_{i+1}|$, which is fixed given $\mathcal H_i$, and
$R_j=\mathbf 1\{j\in A_{i+1}\}$.

\begin{lemma}[Monotone retention]\label{lem:mono}
Fix $\mathcal H_i$, the other draws $T_{i,-j}$, and the tie-breaking randomness. Then for
$j\in A_i$, $R_j$ is a non-decreasing function of $T_{ij}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Raise $T_{ij}$ by $\delta>0$. By~\eqref{eq:ema}, $Q_{ij}$ rises by $\alpha\delta$. Other
active participants' scores are unchanged. A percentile is non-decreasing and 1-Lipschitz
in each coordinate, so $f_P(T_{i,A_i})$ rises by at most $\delta$, and each inactive score
rises by at most $\alpha\delta$. (At epoch $0$ every score is initialised from its first
update, so $Q_{ij}$ rises by $\delta$ and inactive scores by at most $\delta$; participants
arriving later are initialised with a score, so this case does not recur.) Participant
$j$'s score therefore rises at least as much as any competitor's, and its rank cannot fall.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:bias}
$\mathbb E[M_1\mid\mathcal H_i]=\mathrm{lat}(A_i)$, and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bias}
\mathbb E[M_2-M_3\mid\mathcal H_i]
=\frac{1}{N'}\sum_{j\in A_i}\mathrm{Cov}\!\left(R_j,\,T_{ij}\mid\mathcal H_i\right)\;\ge\;0,
\end{equation}
with equality if and only if, almost surely, no retention decision depends on the
participant's own draw.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The first claim is linearity: given $\mathcal H_i$, each $T_{ij}$ has mean
$\tilde Q_j$. For the second, $M_2-M_3=\frac1{N'}\sum_{j\in A_{i+1}}(\tilde T_{ij}-\tilde Q_j)$.
A promoted participant's draw is independent of everything else and contributes zero in
expectation. A participant $j\in A_i$ contributes $R_j(T_{ij}-\tilde Q_j)$, whose
expectation is $\mathrm{Cov}(R_j,T_{ij}\mid\mathcal H_i)$. Condition further on
$T_{i,-j}$ and the tie-breaking randomness. By Lemma~\ref{lem:mono}, $R_j$ is a
non-decreasing function of the single variable $T_{ij}$. A non-decreasing function of a
random variable is non-negatively correlated with it~\cite{esary1967association}, and the
inequality is strict unless the function is a.s.\ constant. Since
$\mathbb E[T_{ij}\mid\mathcal H_i,T_{i,-j}]=\tilde Q_j$ does not depend on the extra
conditioning, the law of total covariance passes the inequality to
$\mathrm{Cov}(R_j,T_{ij}\mid\mathcal H_i)$.
\end{proof}

Two consequences frame everything that follows. First, the bias is carried entirely by
participants at the selection \emph{margin}, whose retention their own draw can flip. If
nobody is at the margin the bias is exactly zero, and empirically it is negligible wherever
turnover is (Section~\ref{sec:churn}).
Second, over a run $M_1$ and $M_3$ estimate the same quantity: their time averages differ
only by the boundary term $[\mathrm{lat}(A_0)-\mathrm{lat}(A_{N_{\mathrm{ep}}})]/N_{\mathrm{ep}}$
and by attrition between selection and the next epoch. Empirically they agree to within
$4\times10^{-4}$ at every $P$ in every pool, whereas $M_2$ departs from both by up to
$0.059$ (Figure~\ref{fig:divergence}).

\subsection{The bias scales with churn, and churn with $P$}
\label{sec:churn}
Proposition~\ref{prop:bias} leaves the magnitude open. Raising $P$ raises the imputed value
credited to inactive participants, so more active participants sit at the margin each
epoch and retention becomes more selective on $T_{ij}$. Pooling all $P$ values and all four
pools, the bias is close to linear in the per-epoch churn rate $c$ (the fraction of the
active set replaced; Figure~\ref{fig:churn}):
\begin{equation}
M_2-M_1\approx 0.108\,c,\qquad R^2=0.874
\end{equation}
(a free-intercept fit gives slope $0.100$ and intercept $0.003$). At $P\le10\%$, churn is
below $0.003$ and the metrics coincide. At $P=100\%$ the bias reaches $0.059$ in the fixed
pool, larger than the mechanism's entire gain over random sortition there ($0.055$).

\subsection{The bias is not specific to percentile imputation}
Proposition~\ref{prop:bias} needs only that retention be monotone in a participant's own
observation. To check that the effect is general, we repeated the audit on a different
mechanism class: an upper-confidence-bound rule~\cite{chen2013combinatorial} that ranks
participants by their running-mean score plus $c\sqrt{\log t/n_j}$ and imputes nothing to
inactive participants. Here the exploration constant $c$ drives turnover, as $P$ did.
Over $c\in[0.02,2]$, $50$ seeds per point, the bias is positive at every setting in every
pool, $M_1$ again matches latent truth, and the bias is linear in churn with slope $0.107$
($R^2=0.91$), against $0.108$ for percentile sortition. The two mechanisms share nothing
but the retention structure, yet the bias per unit of churn is the same. The damage again
follows turnover. Where $c$ drives churn, tuning it on $M_2$ selects far too much
exploration: $c=2$ instead of $0.2$ in the fixed pool and $c=1$ instead of $0.2$ in the
shrinking pool, forfeiting $24\%$ and $25\%$ of the rule's gain over random sortition.
Where churn stays low (growing pool: below $0.03$ at every $c$), the two metrics pick the
same $c$.

