\section{What the Bias Invalidates}
\label{sec:consequences}

\subsection{The optimal percentile, and the cost of choosing it wrongly}
Table~\ref{tab:pstar} reports the quality-maximising $P$ under each metric. Under $M_1$ the
optimum is $20$--$30\%$ in every pool, inside the range of~\cite{kruijssen2024}. Under $M_2$
it is $70$--$85\%$, a regime in which, as~\cite{kruijssen2024} observe, ``the active set is
populated almost randomly''. The practical cost is large. An operator who tunes $P$ on $M_2$
and deploys it obtains a true quality (measured by $M_1$) that forfeits $35$--$48\%$ of the
mechanism's gain over random sortition. Because the optima in Table~\ref{tab:pstar} are
chosen and scored on the same seeds, we replicated the comparison on the independent seeds
of Section~\ref{sec:inversion}, using $P=70\%$ and the recommended $P$ from that grid.
The forfeited share there is $33$--$44\%$.

