\subsection{The ranking of mechanisms inverts}
\label{sec:inversion}
The error does not stay inside one scalar. Figure~\ref{fig:inversion} scores seven
mechanisms on both metrics. In every one of the four pools, the two high-turnover
mechanisms, static $P=70\%$ and a volatility-adaptive rule operating over $[55,85]\%$ (the
range our first-pass analysis had favoured), rank first and second under $M_2$ and beat
the recommended $P=25\%$ significantly ($d=+0.42$ to $+1.51$, $n=100$). Under $M_1$ the same
two rank last apart from random sortition ($d=-0.63$ to $-2.42$). The two are also
indistinguishable from each other under either metric ($|d|\le0.06$). The adaptive rule's
apparent advantage was therefore entirely an effect of running at a higher average $P$,
flattered by a metric that rewards turnover.

