\section{Volatility-Adaptive $P$ Is Equivalent to Fixed $P$}
\label{sec:vams}

\subsection{Hypothesis and protocol}
Volatility-adaptive merit sortition (VAMS) lets the percentile respond to the dispersion
of the active set,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:vams}
P_t=P_{\min}+(P_{\max}-P_{\min})\,\varsigma\!\left(k\,(\sigma_{A,t}-\bar\sigma_{A,t})\right),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma_{A,t}$ is the standard deviation of the active set's observations,
$\bar\sigma_{A,t}$ its mean over the last $W$ epochs, and $\varsigma$ the logistic
function. The intent is to replace more aggressively when the network is unstable. Our
default is $[P_{\min},P_{\max}]=[10,40]\%$, $k=20$, $W=50$, which centres $P_t$ on the
recommended $25\%$.

All comparisons below use $M_1$, $n=100$ seeds per arm, and two one-sided tests against a
smallest effect size of interest $\varepsilon=0.005$, which is $10\%$ of the
merit-over-random gap. ``Equivalent'' means the $90\%$ confidence interval of the
difference lies inside $\pm\varepsilon$. When we claim that a whole family of variants is
equivalent, each member must pass its own test; this intersection--union construction needs
no multiplicity correction.

\subsection{Result}
Table~\ref{tab:vams} compares the default rule with a fixed $P=25\%$. It is equivalent in
all four pools, under $M_1$ and also under $M_2$. Unlike the high-$P$ range above, this
rule operates where the two metrics nearly agree.

