\subsection{Robustness: gain, memory, response curve, drift}
One rule is a weak test of a hypothesis. We therefore swept the sigmoid gain
$k\in\{5,\dots,1000\}$ and window $W\in\{10,50,200\}$, where large $k$ turns the rule into
bang-bang control between $P_{\min}$ and $P_{\max}$. We also tested five further response
curves (linear, exponential, power-law, hard threshold, $\tanh$) at their default
parameters. That is $23$ variants in each of four pools, $n=100$ paired seeds each.

Every one of the $92$ variant--pool pairs is equivalent to a fixed rule set at the
variant's own realised mean percentile (largest $|\Delta|=0.0029$). Measured against the
default $P=25\%$, $86$ of $92$ are equivalent. The six exceptions are the exponential and
power-law curves, which sit at a mean $P_t$ of $36$--$38\%$ and are \emph{worse} than
$P=25\%$ by $0.004$--$0.006$ in three pools. These are the two forms our first-pass
analysis, scored on $M_2$, had ranked first and second. That analysis correctly noticed
that both operate at a higher average $P$, and concluded that the recommended $25\%$
undershoots the optimum. That is exactly the conclusion the biased metric manufactures.

\paragraph{Drift.} In the base model latent quality is fixed, so one can object that VAMS
had nothing to adapt to. We therefore let latent quality follow a mean-reverting
Ornstein--Uhlenbeck process, with the stationary variance held at $\sigma_Q^2$ so that
drift is not confounded with a widening quality distribution. At three drift rates
$\theta\in\{0.005,0.02,0.1\}$ in the fixed and evolving pools ($n=100$, paired), both the
default sigmoid rule and the most aggressive form (a hard threshold) remain equivalent to
fixed $P=25\%$ in all $12$ comparisons ($|\Delta|\le0.003$, TOST $p\le0.009$). Some
differences are detectable but negligible (sigmoid up to $+0.002$, threshold down to
$-0.003$).

The informative part is what VAMS misses. Drift \emph{does} create room for improvement:
the best fixed percentile rises with the drift rate, from $30\%$ at $\theta=0.005$ to at
least $40\%$, the top of our grid, at $\theta=0.1$. There, fixed $P=40\%$ beats $P=25\%$ by
$0.020$ in the evolving pool and $0.015$ in the fixed pool. Yet VAMS's realised mean $P_t$
stays between $24.6$ and $25.0$. The reason is that drift moves participants' means,
while the stationary dispersion of the active set, which is all $\sigma_{A,t}$ measures,
does not change. The failure lies in the signal VAMS adapts to, not in the idea of
adapting.

Figure~\ref{fig:meanP} explains the pattern. In the top row, each variant is plotted at its
realised mean $P_t$ over the curve traced by fixed $P$, and the variants lie on that curve:
a variant does better or worse than $P=25\%$ exactly as much as a fixed rule at the same
average would. The bottom row removes the average and plots what remains against how
strongly each variant adapts. Even when $P_t$ swings with a standard deviation of $15$
points, which is bang-bang control between $10\%$ and $40\%$, the residual stays inside
$\pm\varepsilon$. If anything, it drifts slightly negative as adaptation strengthens
(correlation $-0.62$; about $-0.001$ per $10$ points of standard deviation): jitter in
$P_t$ costs a little and buys nothing. The adaptive component contributes nothing beyond
the average it induces.

