\subsection{Why: the control signal is estimator noise}
For $n$ observations with standard deviation $s$, the sampling standard deviation of the
sample standard deviation is approximately $s/\sqrt{2n}$. Table~\ref{tab:noise} compares
this floor with the measured epoch-to-epoch variability of $\sigma_{A,t}$ under fixed
$P=25\%$. In every pool the signal fluctuates at the floor (ratio $1.01$--$1.03$, apart
from an artefact noted in the table), and its lag-1 autocorrelation is indistinguishable
from zero ($\le0.03$). There is no persistent component for a controller to track. The
reason is structural. Merit selection compresses the latent spread of the active set to
about $0.07$--$0.08$, against observation noise $V=0.2$, so $\sigma_{A,t}^2\approx V^2+\mathrm{Var}(\tilde Q\mid A)$ is dominated by
the noise term. Equation~\eqref{eq:vams} therefore steers $P_t$ with a quantity that is,
to measurement precision, independent noise.

