\section{A Hindsight Bound on Two-Phase Schedules}
\label{sec:headroom}

Refuting rules one at a time invites the objection that a better rule exists. A partial
answer is to ask how much \emph{any} time-varying $P$ of a given form could gain, with
hindsight no online rule has. We search two-phase schedules, $P_t=p_1$ for $t<t_0$ and
$p_2$ afterwards, with $p_1,p_2\in\{10,15,20,25,30,40,60\}\%$ and $t_0\in\{50,150,400\}$.
That is $133$ schedules, including the $7$ constants. We select the best schedule and the
best constant on $50$ tuning seeds and report two numbers. The \emph{oracle gain} is their
difference on those same seeds. Because the schedule set contains the constants and
selection over many noisy candidates only inflates the winner, this is an upper bound on
what a schedule of this form can gain. The \emph{held-out gain} re-scores both picks on
$100$ disjoint seeds, paired.

\pending{headroom results table + text}

This bounds a specific, open-loop family. It does not rule out closed-loop policies that
respond to events in a way no fixed schedule can. It is also specific to the base model,
with fixed latent quality: Section~\ref{sec:vams} shows that latent drift moves the optimal
$P$, so a drift-aware rule could gain. What the bound shows is that, in the base model, the
slow change in pool composition that a schedule can track offers no room for improvement.

