\section{An Evaluation Protocol for Selection Mechanisms}
\label{sec:protocol}

Each failure we encountered is cheap to prevent once named. We recommend the following
for any study that evaluates a mechanism which selects agents on noisy observations.

\paragraph{1. Score before you select.} Measure the population that \emph{delivered} the
epoch's work, before the selection rule has used those observations ($M_1$). If a
post-selection quantity is needed, score it on fresh observations only. Any quantity that
reuses the observations that drove a selection is conditioned on it
(Proposition~\ref{prop:bias}).

\paragraph{2. Check against latent truth.} In simulation the latent qualities are known,
so computing $M_3$ costs one line of code. A disagreement between the reported metric and
$M_3$ is the most direct test for this class of bug. It would have caught ours on the
first run.

\paragraph{3. Ask whether the bias moves with the treatment.} A constant offset is
harmless for ranking. A bias that scales with turnover, exploration rate, or any other
quantity the treatment changes is not. Plot the metric's gap to ground truth against the
treatment variable (Figure~\ref{fig:churn}).

\paragraph{4. Separate adaptation from its average.} An adaptive rule changes both how a
parameter responds and where it sits on average. Compare it with a static rule fixed at
its \emph{realised mean} parameter, not only with the default (Figure~\ref{fig:meanP}).

\paragraph{5. Tune and test on different seeds.} Hyperparameters chosen on the evaluation
seeds inherit the optimizer's curse~\cite{smith2006optimizer,cawley2010overfitting}. Use
disjoint tuning seeds, and paired comparisons on common seeds for power.

\paragraph{6. State nulls as equivalence.} Fix a smallest effect size of interest in
advance and report two one-sided tests~\cite{lakens2017equivalence}. At $n=10$ seeds per
arm, a two-sample test has $80\%$ power only for $d\ge1.33$ ($1.51$ at $n=8$), so a non-significant
result at that sample size is nearly uninformative in either direction.

