\section{Discussion and Limitations}

\paragraph{Why this bias is easy to make and hard to see.} $M_2$ is not an exotic
construction. ``What is the quality of the set we just chose?'' is a natural question for an
operator, and a dashboard that answers it from each member's latest score computes exactly
$M_2$. The bias is invisible in any single run, has the right sign to look like an
improvement, and grows with precisely the quantities, such as exploration and turnover,
that new mechanisms tend to increase. Any mechanism that raises churn is flattered, and an
evaluation that rewards churn will steer a research programme toward it, as ours was
steered toward high-$P$ adaptive rules.

\paragraph{What remains open.} Our null results are about a specific idea: adapting a
\emph{scalar} percentile to the active set's dispersion. Two directions survive. First,
drift moves the optimal $P$ (Section~\ref{sec:vams}), so a controller driven by a signal
that actually tracks drift, rather than dispersion, might gain where VAMS cannot. Second,
the scalar itself may be the bottleneck. Making the optimism per participant changes the
mechanism class, and we evaluate that direction, under the protocol of
Section~\ref{sec:protocol}, in separate work.

\paragraph{Limitations.} All results are simulations in the model
of~\cite{kruijssen2024}, with Gaussian observation noise and, except in the drift
experiment, latent quality fixed for life. Proposition~\ref{prop:bias} requires retention to
be monotone in a participant's own observation. This holds for top-$k$ selection on an EMA
or on a mean plus a count-based bonus, but a rule that, for example, penalises a
participant's own observed variance could violate it. The smallest effect size of interest,
$10\%$ of the merit-over-random gap, is a judgement; all intervals are reported so readers
can apply their own. The headroom bound covers two-phase open-loop schedules on a finite
grid, not all adaptive policies. Finally, the biased metric was our own construction; we
report it as a case study of a general failure mode, not as an error in prior work.

\section{Conclusion}

Scoring a selected set with the observations that selected it produces a bias that is
provably non-negative, grows with turnover, and therefore tracks any parameter that
controls turnover. In merit-based sortition this bias moved the apparent optimal
replacement percentile from $20$--$30\%$ to $70$--$85\%$, cost up to $48\%$ of the
mechanism's benefit when acted on, and inverted the ranking of competing mechanisms. The
same bias, at the same rate per unit of churn, appears in an unrelated bandit-style
selection rule. Measured correctly, volatility-adaptive replacement is equivalent to a
fixed percentile, because its control signal is estimator noise. The remedy is simple and
general: score before you select, and check against ground truth whenever you can.

